      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can you go into a bit more detail on the specific chemical mechanisms? like why does the solubility change so drastically as the pH crosses those thresholds? i want to make sure the client understands the actual science behind why they might see deficiencies if the pH drifts.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The drastic change in solubility of micronutrients like iron and manganese with pH is largely due to the chemistry of metal-hydride and organometallic interactions. At higher pH levels (above 7), the formation of hydroxide ions (OH-) promotes the solubilization of Fe3+ complexes, such as ferric ethylenethiamine, by suppressing the formation of insoluble hydroxide-absorbed Fe3+ species. Conversely, at lower pH levels (below 5.5), the formation of hydroxide ions also sequesters Mn2+, forming insoluble hydroxide-absorbed Mn2+ species.\n\nAdditionally, the metal-hydride interactions can affect solubility by stabilizing or destabilizing unions with other molecules, such as carbonates, oxides, and EDTA. However, organic chelation complexation and microbial uptake can stress micronutrient availability.",
